WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:11.750
[Music] If you think that&nbsp;
earning money is the only goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:17.666
you will end up doing things every day&nbsp;
that you don't like doing to earn money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.666 --> 00:00:23.083
and to live a life doing things you don't&nbsp;
like is such a ridiculous thing to do.

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:31.666
Nearly two hundred thousand people arrive&nbsp;
in Australia every year as migrants. [Music]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.666 --> 00:00:37.291
One-third of the small businesses are owned&nbsp;
by migrants. We wanted to find out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:42.500
makes them do what they do, what is their&nbsp;
motivation, what challenges do they face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:48.625
and after all these systemic odds and&nbsp;
hassles, is it really worth it? [Music]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:02.750
I think it takes a certain kind of person&nbsp;
who's got a real tolerance for risk to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:08.000
entrepreneur or just kind of really wants to have&nbsp;
that to own their own narrative. Everything around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.750
you, look around you, every single thing that&nbsp;
you're wearing, every single piece of furniture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.333
around you, every single building around&nbsp;
you, they've all been made by entrepreneurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:23.333
Entrepreneurs are nothing but creators, right? So&nbsp;
we need creators in this world because only people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:29.916
who create can make the world a better place.&nbsp;
An entrepreneur is a very different animal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:34.250
I would say, someone who thinks differently,&nbsp;
someone who sees the world differently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:39.833
As an entrepreneur, you will have a&nbsp;
very unique way of looking at things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:45.291
Melbourne has one of the most vibrant&nbsp;
multicultural communities in Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:53.916
and we were about to meet four entrepreneurs&nbsp;
to hear their stories. [Music] [Applause]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.916 --> 00:02:02.458
One day my boss told me, "Drop everything, stop&nbsp;
everything, come with me. You need to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.333
why we do what we do." She brought me to&nbsp;
this beach cleanup, but the organizers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:14.458
actually didn't want to call it a cleanup; it&nbsp;
was a beach audit. True enough, after an hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:20.541
after we cleaned the beach, it was dirty again.&nbsp;
You know, that's when it hit me how big this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.541 --> 00:02:28.250
problem of packaging waste was. Ultimately,&nbsp;
I came here with a huge mission, which was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:35.000
figure out a solution to this packaging waste&nbsp;
problem. I think it was sometime in 2018 when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.416 --> 00:02:44.458
like everybody else, I was in a nine-to-five,&nbsp;
still dancing but under my Guru, but always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.458 --> 00:02:52.791
wanting to understand how I can do something more&nbsp;
than this. I was trying to find more purpose in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.791 --> 00:03:01.166
life. For me to pursue entrepreneurship was always&nbsp;
a one-point decision. I see the possibilities when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.166 --> 00:03:06.208
it comes to entrepreneurship, and something that&nbsp;
really keeps me awake, you know, in a good way.

00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:13.833
But only if it is that simple, there is no&nbsp;
doubt about entrepreneurs having to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:18.583
from zero. But being a migrant entrepreneur&nbsp;
often means you have to begin below zero.

00:03:21.458 --> 00:03:26.750
A number of people I've met, including myself, we&nbsp;
have to start from scratch. Nobody knows nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:32.500
It's like you're born again. I mean, in terms of&nbsp;
challenges as a migrant founder, I think a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:39.833
direct one is that the migrant journey is designed&nbsp;
for employees. You know, the barriers to entry are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:46.000
hard enough for entrepreneurs. It's a really hard,&nbsp;
um, and it's really hard to have success as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.250
entrepreneur, let alone if you're a migrant. You&nbsp;
know, you've already got language barriers. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:57.333
potentially talking a little bit of xenophobia,&nbsp;
which still probably exists in Australia. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:01.041
was raised with two sisters, you know, so I've&nbsp;
technically had three mothers, you know. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:06.041
even at my home, if I want to drink or what else&nbsp;
I want to drink water in it's just being served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.041 --> 00:04:14.208
to me, you know, and here I am mopping a floor in&nbsp;
a foreign world, and particularly you feel people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.208 --> 00:04:18.458
are walking in with the suits and everything, you&nbsp;
know, because I was in a CBD district and near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:22.791
Collins Street, so some of your community people&nbsp;
as well, very well dressed up, and they come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.791 --> 00:04:29.125
through and you feel I was just embarrassed to do&nbsp;
that kind of work, you know. I mean, I came over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:33.833
here from England eight years ago. I was a lawyer&nbsp;
back in the UK and a captain in the British Army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:39.500
and I ended up picking fruits, doing door to door sales&nbsp;
because I had to start from the beginning...almost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:44.375
And so, for entrepreneurs, I think that's&nbsp;
why a lot of migrants come into the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.375 --> 00:04:48.333
get drawn into entrepreneurship because&nbsp;
you can go and build something yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.416 --> 00:04:54.583
and you can kind of own your own narrative,&nbsp;
whereas if you want to try drop into the job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.583 --> 00:05:01.250
landscape, you kind of got to reset. I think&nbsp;
having the willingness to just talk and open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.708
in front of us, that would be the first thing.&nbsp;
So again, in our business, we are not selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.708 --> 00:05:12.583
a product. You go and just do a transaction, ours&nbsp;
is a relationship business. Before we do business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:18.041
people need to know us, people need to like&nbsp;
us, and then people need to trust us before any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.041 --> 00:05:23.916
business happens. Just having that opportunity for&nbsp;
people to open up is not that straightforward. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.916 --> 00:05:29.083
of the challenges as a business owner was that&nbsp;
acceptance. I think I found that journey a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.083 --> 00:05:34.541
challenging because just like anybody else, you&nbsp;
always want to be accepted, you've got to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.541 --> 00:05:39.291
trust of the people, so that took a while because&nbsp;
it's not just the clients that we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.291 --> 00:05:46.125
about; it's also the community. So re-establishing&nbsp;
myself back in that landscape was challenging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:56.541
It's like sometimes we feel that we get&nbsp;
more criticized or analyzed by our own race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:04.125
Social challenges may be a subjective problem, but&nbsp;
financial support is very much an objective issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.708 --> 00:06:09.708
Disparities are still persistent; the&nbsp;
overall system needs many changes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.708 --> 00:06:20.125
make it supportive for entrepreneurs. For&nbsp;
example, before 2021, the rules for visas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.125 --> 00:06:30.166
for entrepreneurs were so impractical that my&nbsp;
immigration agent told me that I was naive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.166 --> 00:06:34.833
ridiculous for even thinking about a startup,&nbsp;
actually said, "Don't waste your time because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:41.333
the whole system wouldn't benefit it." So, I'm&nbsp;
really close to a founder at the minute; she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.916 --> 00:06:46.958
is a first-generation migrant from the&nbsp;
Philippines, and her family moved here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.541 --> 00:06:54.208
I think, 30, 40 years ago, and, you know,&nbsp;
she was born here but is really facing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.208 --> 00:07:04.375
challenges both as a female and as a migrant and a&nbsp;
person of color in terms of starting her business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.375 --> 00:07:12.083
So, she's currently fundraising, and finding all&nbsp;
that kind of unconscious bias is really impacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.083 --> 00:07:16.708
her journey as an entrepreneur. I think for&nbsp;
her, it's, you know, we, with so much work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.708 --> 00:07:21.958
around that diversity space in terms of gender,&nbsp;
which is amazing, it needs to change the dial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.916 --> 00:07:28.833
um, but she thinks that for her personally, it's&nbsp;
the bigger challenge is not being a woman; it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.916 --> 00:07:38.875
her background in terms of where she's from. A big&nbsp;
challenge for us right now is financial support;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:43.291
it's been a struggle trying to&nbsp;
get funding, even though there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.291 --> 00:07:48.083
are those systems that technically&nbsp;
exist. Yeah, yeah, does that work?

00:07:50.208 --> 00:07:55.541
It's a good question, that does it work, because&nbsp;
it works, which means does funding happen, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.541 --> 00:08:00.750
funding happens, but it's who does the funding&nbsp;
go to, right, and where does the, where do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:06.166
people actually get the information or where&nbsp;
do they get the support? This year at funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.166 --> 00:08:10.791
in terms of investment into female-founded&nbsp;
businesses, was two percent of overall funding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.791 --> 00:08:16.041
Now, in Startup Victoria, our membership is&nbsp;
42 percent female, and we're generally a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.041 --> 00:08:19.958
early stage organization because that's&nbsp;
when founders need the most community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:25.416
so how does 42 percent translate to&nbsp;
two percent of the funding, there's

00:08:25.416 --> 00:08:30.083
obviously somewhere where there's a huge&nbsp;
disparity happening. It is a brave thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.083 --> 00:08:34.833
start a business, but just because you're good&nbsp;
at something does not mean you will be good at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.833 --> 00:08:39.958
running the business. One of the things I think&nbsp;
most founders don't do is really understand why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.958 --> 00:08:44.875
they're building a business. If you understand&nbsp;
the why behind your building a business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.875 --> 00:08:49.500
it becomes really apparent to you what you&nbsp;
want to do and what you want out of it. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:54.583
quite regularly brilliant people with awesome&nbsp;
ideas, great innovators, but they don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.583 --> 00:09:00.708
the commercial background in terms of how to do&nbsp;
business, those areas, especially, we see a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.291 --> 00:09:05.916
people who struggle to commercialize their&nbsp;
business and actually make it successful beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.916 --> 00:09:11.875
the idea stage. In order to maintain the right&nbsp;
balance between goals, risks, and opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.875 --> 00:09:18.500
having a realistic mindset is necessary. I think&nbsp;
there are two sides to the coin when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:24.583
migrants. They've actually gone ahead and proven&nbsp;
that they're resilient, they're adaptable. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.583 --> 00:09:28.916
these are all qualities of a great entrepreneur&nbsp;
anyway, right? So, they've already got these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.916 --> 00:09:33.416
qualities, they've proven these qualities&nbsp;
already. We tend to overestimate the negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.416 --> 00:09:41.208
impact of what could be, and we totally forget&nbsp;
or maybe underestimate what good that can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.208 --> 00:09:48.500
And so, when we tell this story that if I take the&nbsp;
plunge, if I do something on my own, I have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:54.833
negative insights into me than the positive&nbsp;
ones, and that sort of makes me do the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.833 --> 00:10:00.166
thing because I'm not taking a balanced view.&nbsp;
This is one thing that I kept telling myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.166 --> 00:10:05.541
and this is one thing a wise man told me, and that&nbsp;
wise man is actually my husband. He said to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.166 --> 00:10:10.000
"There is an audience for Madonna,&nbsp;
there is an audience for Beyoncé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.833
there is an audience for Taylor Swift, there&nbsp;
will be an audience for Chandralaya." And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.833 --> 00:10:21.625
something that really stuck to me because not&nbsp;
everybody gets attracted to what you're offering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.375 --> 00:10:28.375
While the right mindset is essential,&nbsp;
personal drive often acts as fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.125 --> 00:10:34.625
because although discipline can keep you going,&nbsp;
only emotions give you that kickstart. [Music]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.708 --> 00:10:42.500
It's almost like you've got a chip on your&nbsp;
shoulder because in two ways, one is you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:49.166
want to prove to the new, the host country, and&nbsp;
you want to prove that you can do something. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.166 --> 00:10:55.041
second part is you left your parent country.&nbsp;
People have different expectations, maybe you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.041 --> 00:11:01.041
have told them why you're leaving, and maybe what&nbsp;
you're doing here is not what you told them why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.041 --> 00:11:07.041
you're leaving. So you also have a point to prove&nbsp;
to them because when I was in uni, I used to walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:13.375
two and a half kilometers to buy my groceries&nbsp;
and walk back with all the groceries because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.208 --> 00:11:20.750
that way I can save money on a ticket&nbsp;
because I can use that money towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:27.416
buying groceries because there were times I&nbsp;
had no money to eat food. And one of my biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.958 --> 00:11:37.416
influences was my mother. I was my mother, still&nbsp;
is my mother, and in 2018 when I decided to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.416 --> 00:11:44.000
the plunge, I got this very bad news that my mum&nbsp;
was diagnosed with breast cancer, and that really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:50.083
pushed me back. So I started to embark on this&nbsp;
journey of starting the school, and I named it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.083 --> 00:11:55.583
after her. Little did I know that I was going to&nbsp;
lose my mother that very same year, so that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.583 --> 00:12:01.791
one of the worst emotional journeys I have felt&nbsp;
in my life because this was a tribute to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.791 --> 00:12:09.708
and I wanted her to see it while she was alive,&nbsp;
and sadly she couldn't. But I know every day she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.291 --> 00:12:17.791
up there looking and really loving this journey&nbsp;
that I've created for myself and for others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.791 --> 00:12:26.458
and it's been such a gratifying experience because&nbsp;
if not for her, this wouldn't have happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.708 --> 00:12:34.958
I'm completely gratified. I think it's one of&nbsp;
the best things I've done for me, for my family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.416 --> 00:12:40.791
and I feel I'm doing this, it's a purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.791 --> 00:12:47.666
it's a very strong purpose that I've got.&nbsp;
Sorry, it's just...no, it's okay.

00:12:56.083 --> 00:12:57.083
[Music]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.083 --> 00:13:00.041
While there are strong reasons&nbsp;
for their entrepreneurial journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.041 --> 00:13:08.750
not everyone is inclined to become a unicorn&nbsp;
story. The rise in economic inequality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:13.041
monopolies, and consolidation is also the story of&nbsp;
multinationals and unicorns. Entrepreneurship is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.041 --> 00:13:18.208
fundamental to a truly democratic society;&nbsp;
the unicorn narrative needs to change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.291 --> 00:13:22.666
Yeah, I think the unicorn&nbsp;
narrative is really damaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.291 --> 00:13:28.125
You know, we meet a lot of founders that&nbsp;
have or entrepreneurs that have an idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.375 --> 00:13:37.125
and they start to come into the ecosystem, and&nbsp;
they straight away think there's either failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.125 --> 00:13:43.666
or becoming a unicorn, and they're the only two&nbsp;
options. Being a unicorn, I think, has become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.666 --> 00:13:50.666
fashion now. There's a lot of things that go into&nbsp;
these aspects, and so being tied to that dream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.666 --> 00:13:55.958
a unicorn or a decagon, I think, makes it very&nbsp;
difficult for people to really see what success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.958 --> 00:14:02.666
means. So defining your success very at the very&nbsp;
start is important and then sort of thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.666 --> 00:14:08.791
what is making it big for you really look like.&nbsp;
I think there's a necessity for more education in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.791 --> 00:14:14.791
what it means to define success for you and how to&nbsp;
have a good strong business without trying to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.416 --> 00:14:23.083
reach too high and falling over. Like there are&nbsp;
lots of unicorns out there that have still yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.083 --> 00:14:30.750
to show how to make a profit even in years into&nbsp;
it, and as Shimroth is saying, they burn so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:35.375
supposedly, to create value, but if the value&nbsp;
that they create is kind of questionable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.375 --> 00:14:41.208
something that's important to us is growing&nbsp;
sustainably, and obviously, we want to make money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.208 --> 00:14:46.708
and we want to be successful, but at the same&nbsp;
time, we want our sustainable impact to be tied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.708 --> 00:14:52.916
to our bottom line, so that's something that's&nbsp;
important as we scale. There are startups and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.916 --> 00:14:58.541
small businesses that are not just about making a&nbsp;
profit; their definition of success is different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.125 --> 00:15:09.166
It is equitable; it is inclusive, collaborating&nbsp;
with art organizations to create meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.166 --> 00:15:15.291
conversations about social economic issues&nbsp;
through art, giving out really strong messaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.958 --> 00:15:22.833
That is success to me. And at the end of the&nbsp;
day, success to me is, am I fulfilled? Is my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.833 --> 00:15:29.750
purpose here fulfilled? So everything I do is&nbsp;
with people I love, people I care for, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.291 --> 00:15:36.958
you know, so to me, all of that needs&nbsp;
significant hard work, you know. So, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.666 --> 00:15:45.708
while that is success, but it's a continuous&nbsp;
journey, you know, and I'm always, I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.708 --> 00:15:51.750
say people need to have something that they are&nbsp;
really chasing, and for me, my big thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:58.583
I'm chasing is always to be better than I was&nbsp;
yesterday, and for that, I need to work today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.541 --> 00:16:06.291
It's not always you have to be a unicorn; you&nbsp;
can be a Zebra company if you want to. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.291 --> 00:16:10.666
small business, sustainable business because end&nbsp;
of the day, I think when you look at the failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:15.875
and fear of it, define what is failure&nbsp;
for you, define what is success for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.875 --> 00:16:21.083
If your business is contributing something in&nbsp;
society, if it is making people's life better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.083 --> 00:16:26.333
you're a success. If you're contributing to&nbsp;
someone else's dream to make them happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.333 --> 00:16:32.625
that's a success. If you're contributing towards&nbsp;
sustainability, doing something for a larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.625 --> 00:16:40.833
ecosystem, it's a success. I think personally&nbsp;
I've grown massively in a mental capacity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.708 --> 00:16:53.625
I look at life so differently now; it's not a&nbsp;
nine-to-five anymore; it's a 24/7. Of how to, and

00:16:53.625 --> 00:17:00.958
24/7 in a very happy way, you know. I'm not&nbsp;
cornering myself and saying, "Oh my God, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.958 --> 00:17:05.500
know what I'm doing, no, oh my God, what is the&nbsp;
next thing we could do to empower these people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:12.458
how can I inject more cultural diversity here?"&nbsp;
All the setbacks, the failures, the emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.458 --> 00:17:19.791
dramas, all of that has really fine-tuned&nbsp;
me as a person, as an artist, as a teacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.791 --> 00:17:27.583
as an entrepreneur. The conversations, the&nbsp;
relationships, I would any day cherish all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.583 --> 00:17:33.083
of that. When we asked a lot of entrepreneurs the&nbsp;
same question, like you have gone through so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.083 --> 00:17:39.041
hassles, so many low moments, high moments,&nbsp;
what do you think, whether it was worth it?

00:17:40.208 --> 00:17:47.375
A very interesting question because from my&nbsp;
experience, like, throughout every milestone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.375 --> 00:17:54.041
on this journey, whether Zerotag will&nbsp;
play out in terms, you know, financially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.041 --> 00:18:00.291
you know, that's something we'd have to see. I&nbsp;
do know that I've made my life so much harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.875 --> 00:18:09.416
